          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em regular epimorphism
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\to d
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            which behaves in the way that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering"} covering
            is expected to behave, in the sense that “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            is the union of the parts of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
          %p
            In a category with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullbacks"} pullbacks
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+epimorphisms"} effective epimorphisms
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+epimorphisms"} strict epimorphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+epimorphism"} split epimorphism
            is regular, but the converses frequently fail.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
          #RegularEpimorphism.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong regular epimorphism
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi c
                    %mo →
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon c \to d
              (in a given
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizer"} coequalizer
              of
              %em some
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+pair"} parallel pair
              of morphisms, i.e. if there exists
              %em some
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
              diagram of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi c
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi f
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi d
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    a
                    \\;\rightrightarrows\;
                    c
                    \\xrightarrow{\;f\;}
                    d.
            %p
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+duality"} dual
              concept to Def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RegularEpimorphism"}
              is that of
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+monomorphism"} regular monomorphism
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#epimorphy Epimorphy
          %p
            Although Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RegularEpimorphism"}
            does not state it explicitly, it is true (and easy to prove) that:
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              Every regular epimorphism (Def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RegularEpimorphism"}
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
              %br/
          In fact, every regular epimorphism is a
          = succeed "," do
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+epimorphism"} strong epimorphism
          = succeed "." do
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extremal+epimorphism"} extremal epimorphism
          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
          must in fact be an
          = succeed "." do
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
          %p
            Frequently (such as in a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback-stability"} pullback-stable
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent+morphism"} descent morphism
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+category"} exact
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+coequalizers"} reflexive coequalizers
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+descent+morphism"} effective descent morphism
          %p
            On the other hand, every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+epimorphism"} split epimorphism
            is regular, but the converse holds only rarely (it is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
            form of the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          %h3#effective_epimorphisms Effective epimorphisms
          %p
            Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RegularEpimorphism"}
            refers only to
            %em some
            parallel pair, but often there is a canonical choice: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel+pair"} kernel pair
            of the morphism in question:
          %p
          #MorphismsWithKernelPairsAreRegularIffEffective.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              A morphism having a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel+pair"} kernel pair
              (such as any morphism in a category with
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullbacks"} pullbacks
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RegularEpimorphism"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizer"} coequalizer
              of its kernel pair.
              %br/
          E.g.
          = succeed "," do
            %a{:href => "#Borceux94I"} Borceux 1994 I, Prop. 2.5.7
          = succeed "." do
            %a{:href => "#Taylor99"} Taylor 1999, Lemma 5.6.6
          = succeed "." do
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+kernels"} generalized kernels
          %p
          #EffectiveEpimorphism.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A regular epimorphism that satisfies the following two conditions (which are equivalent, by Prop.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MorphismsWithKernelPairsAreRegularIffEffective"}
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  it has a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel+pair"} kernel pair
              %li
                %p
                  it is the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizer"} coequalizer
                  of its kernel pair
            %p
              is called an
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+epimorphism"} effective epimorphism
          %h3#pullbacks_and_pushouts Pullbacks and pushouts
          %p
          #InRegularCategoryRegularEpisAreStableUnderPullback.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              In a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+epimorphisms"} regular epimorphisms
              are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preserved+limit"} preserved
              by
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
          This is by definition of “
          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"}> regular category
          ”.
          %p
          #InRegularCategoryEffectiveEpisAreStableUnderPullback.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (in regular category pullback preserves effective epimorphisms)
              %br/
              In a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+epimorphisms"} effective epimorphisms
              (Def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EffectiveEpimorphism"}
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            %p
              Given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commuting diagram
              of the form
            %svg{:height => "149.971", :viewbox => "0 0 112.057 149.971", :width => "112.057", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
