            %p where
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  the right vertical column is both a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel+pair"} kernel pair
                  and a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizer"} coequalizer
              %li
                %p
                  all parallel squares are
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullbacks"} pullbacks
            %p then also the left vertical column is both a kernel pair and a coequalizer, hence the kernel pair of a regular epimorphism.
          (
          %a{:href => "#Borceux94II"}> Borceux 1994 II, Prop. 2.3.3
          )
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              The left column is again a kernel pair since
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits+commute+with+limits"} limits commute with limits
              and the left bottom vertical morphism is again a regular epimorphism, by Prop.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InRegularCategoryRegularEpisAreStableUnderPullback"}
            %p
              Therefore the left vertical column is also a coequalizer by Prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MorphismsWithKernelPairsAreRegularIffEffective"}
              and hence an effective epimorphism by Def.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EffectiveEpimorphism"}
          #PullbackSquareOfRegularEpisIsPushout.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              In a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↙
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp; \swarrow &amp; \searrow
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \searrow &amp; \swarrow
                    }
            %p
              the two bottom morphisms are regular epis (hence by prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InRegularCategoryRegularEpisAreStableUnderPullback"}
              all four morphisms are), then the diagram is also a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
          %p
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Johnstone"} Johnstone, section A, prop. 1.4.3
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              In a regular category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+epimorphisms"} strong epimorphisms
              (=
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extremal+epimorphisms"} extremal epimorphisms
              since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              admits pullbacks) coincide; hence regular epimorphisms are closed under composition.
          %p
            (See
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "#Johnstone"} Johnstone, A.1.3.4
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Regular epimorphisms are not generally closed under composition.
              %a{:href => "#Taylor99"} Taylor 1999 p. 289
              gives the following example in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝟚
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{2}
              be the
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+category"} interval category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mi 𝟚
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \rightrightarrows \mathbb{2}
              is a regular epi
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝟚
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{2} \to B\mathbb{N}
              (the codomain is the additive monoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              made into a 1-object category; the epi sends the non-identity arrow of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝟚
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{2}
              to
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \in \mathbb{N}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{Z}/3
              of monoids. But the composite
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝟚
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                        %mi e
                    %mi B
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{2} \to B\mathbb{N} \stackrel{B e}{\to} B\mathbb{Z}/3
            %p
              is not a regular epi in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#Bed"} Bednarczyk et al.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} the category of sets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Set
                to other categories, what kind of epimorphism to use. Frequently, regular epimorphisms are a good choice. In particular, one may define a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} projective object
                (and hence the
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %li
              %p
                More generally, in every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
                every epimorphism is regular (e.g.
                %a{:href => "#Borceux94III"} Borceux 1994III, Prop. 3.4.13 on p. 218
                and in fact
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+epimorphism"} effective
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} the category of groups
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/toddtrimble/published/epimorphisms+in+the+category+of+groups"} here
            %li
              %p
                In the category of
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}\hookrightarrow\mathbb{Z}
                is an epimorphism, even though it is far from a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+category"} algebraic category
                (a category of models of an
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}\hookrightarrow\mathbb{Z}
                is not a regular epimorphism.
            %li
              %p
                Similarly, in the category of rings, not every epimorphism is regular, as shown by the example
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi ℚ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z} \hookrightarrow \mathbb{Q}
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+epimorphism"} split epimorphism
                is regular, and split epimorphisms are badly behaved in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Diff
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Diff
                (for example, pullbacks of split epis do not necessarily exist), the usual procedure is to consider the largest class of arrows inside regular epis of which all pullbacks exist, namely the surjective
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submersions"} submersions
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+stacks"} differentiable stacks
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoids"} Lie groupoids
                it is surjective submersions that play the role of regular epimorphisms.
          %h2#related_pages Related pages
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The page
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
                has a list of many types of epimorphism and their relationships.
            %li
              %p
                The dual concept is
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+monomorphism"} regular monomorphism
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+epimorphisms"} effective epimorphisms
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+epimorphisms"} strict epimorphisms
                are the same as regular ones.
            %li
              %p In higher category theory:
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+epimorphism+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} regular epimorphism in an (∞,1)-category
          %h2#references References
          %p Textbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jiri+Adamek"}> Jiří Adámek
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Horst+Herrlich"} Horst Herrlich
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/George+Strecker"} George Strecker
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Abstract+and+Concrete+Categories+--+The+Joy+of+Cats"} Abstract and Concrete Categories – The Joy of Cats
                John Wiley and Sons, New York (1990) reprinted as: Reprints in Theory and Applications of Categories
                %strong 17
                (2006) 1-507 (
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/17/tr17abs.html"}> tac:tr17
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://katmat.math.uni-bremen.de/acc/"} book webpage
            %li#Borceux94I
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                , Def. 4.3.1 in:
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Categorical+Algebra"} Handbook of Categorical Algebra
                Vol. 1:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Basic Category Theory
                %strong 50
                Cambridge University Press (1994) (
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511525858"}> doi:10.1017/CBO9780511525858
                )
            %li#Borceux94II
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                , Chapter 2 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Categorical+Algebra"} Handbook of Categorical Algebra
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categories and Structures
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 50
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511525865"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511525865
            %li#Borceux94III
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                , Section 3.3.4 of:
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Categorical+Algebra"} Handbook of Categorical Algebra
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categories of Sheaves
                %strong 50
                Cambridge University Press (1994) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511525872"}> doi:10.1017/CBO9780511525872
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Exposition and examples:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Qiaochu+Yuan"}> Qiaochu Yuan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://qchu.wordpress.com/2012/11/03/regular-and-effective-monomorphisms-and-epimorphisms/"} Regular and effective monomorphisms and epimorphisms
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li#Johnstone
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                , Section A of:
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sketches+of+an+Elephant"} Sketches of an Elephant
            %li#Bed
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marek+A.+Bednarczyk"}> Marek A. Bednarczyk
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrzej+M.+Borzyszkowski"} Andrzej M. Borzyszkowski
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wieslaw+Pawlowski"} Wieslaw Pawlowski
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Generalized congruences – epimorphisms in
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mi a
                          %mi t
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}a t
                %strong 5
                11 (1999) 266-280 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/1999/n11/5-11abs.html"}> tac:5-11
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://eudml.org/doc/120226?lang=fr&limit=5"} dml:120226
            %li#Taylor99
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Taylor"}> Paul Taylor
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
